------------------------
Locators in Playwright
------------------------

A locator is used to identify and interact with an element on a web page.

For example, if we want to click a button:
await page.locator('#login').click();

Here:

#login
   ↓
Locator
   ↓
Identifies the login element

CSS Selector vs XPath in Playwright

------------------------
Playwright supports different ways of locating elements, including:
------------------------

CSS Selectors
XPath
Text
Playwright-specific locators such as getByRole(), getByText(), getByLabel(), etc.

For basic CSS locator usage, we can use the following syntax.

------------------------
1. If id is present
------------------------

Suppose HTML is:
<input id="username">

CSS Syntax
#id

or:
tagname#id

Examples
await page.locator('#username');

or:
await page.locator('input#username');

Both identify the same element.

Easy way to remember
# → ID

------------------------
2. If class attribute is present
------------------------

Suppose HTML is:
<input class="username">

CSS Syntax
.class

or:
tagname.class

Examples
await page.locator('.username');

or:
await page.locator('input.username');

Easy way to remember
. → Class

------------------------
3. CSS Traversing — Parent to Child
------------------------

Sometimes we need to locate a child element inside a parent element.

Example:

<div>
    <button>Login</button>
</div>

We can locate the button using the parent-child relationship.

Syntax
parenttagname >> childtagname

Example:
await page.locator('div >> button');

Meaning:
div
 ↓
button

So Playwright searches for the button inside the div.

Note: Modern CSS selectors can also express parent-to-descendant relationships using a space, 
such as div button. Playwright also supports the >> selector syntax, but for new code, 
Playwright's recommended locators such as getByRole() 
are generally preferable when they provide a good semantic target.

------------------------
4. Locator Based on Text
------------------------

Sometimes we want to locate an element based on the text displayed on the page.

For example:
<button>Login</button>

We can use:
await page.getByText('Login');

or, when using Playwright's CSS/text selector syntax:
await page.locator('text=Login');

Example
await page.getByText('Login').click();

--------------------------------
SINGLE QUOTE ' VS DOUBLE QUOTE "
--------------------------------

Both can be used to write strings in JavaScript/TypeScript.

'text'
"text"

Both mean the same thing.


Example:

await page.locator('#username');

await page.locator("#username");

Both are valid.


WHY DO WE SOMETIMES SEE BOTH?

It depends on which quote is already present inside the string.

Example:

page.locator("[style*='block']")

Here:

"        → Outer quote
'block'  → Inner quote

This is convenient because the inner and outer quotes are different.


Another example:

page.locator('[style*="block"]')

Here:

'        → Outer quote
"block"  → Inner quote

This is also valid.


EASY RULE TO REMEMBER:

'text'
    OR
"text"

Both are valid.

When you have quotes inside the locator, use different
outer and inner quotes to make the string easier to write.


Example:

"[style*='block']"   ✅
'[style*="block"]'   ✅


IMPORTANT:

The quotes are mainly JavaScript/TypeScript string syntax.
They are not part of the CSS selector itself.

CSS selector:
[style*='block']

JavaScript string containing that selector:
"[style*='block']"

------------------------
fill() vs type() in Playwright
------------------------

Both methods are used to enter text into input fields.

1. fill()

fill() enters the complete text at once.
await page.locator('#username').fill('Shiva');

Meaning:
Input field
    ↓
Shiva

It also clears the existing value first and then enters the new value.

2. type()

type() enters the text character by character, similar to typing on a keyboard.
await page.locator('#username').type('Shiva');

Conceptually:
S → h → i → v → a

You can also add a delay between characters:
await page.locator('#username').type('Shiva', { delay: 100 });

Main Difference

| `fill()`                             | `type()`                                          |
| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Enters text quickly                  | Types character by character                      |
| Clears existing value first          | Does not automatically clear existing value       |
| Generally preferred for normal input | Useful when you specifically need typing behavior |

For normal Playwright automation, fill() is usually the preferred method for entering input values.

