--------------------------------
SELECTING SPECIFIC ELEMENTS USING .first(), .nth(), .last() AND .allTextContents()
--------------------------------

When a locator matches multiple elements, Playwright provides
.first(), .nth(), and .last() to select a specific element.

.allTextContents() can be used to get the text from ALL matching elements.

Example:

console.log(await page.locator(".card-body a").first().textContent());

console.log(await page.locator(".card-body a").nth(1).textContent());

console.log(await page.locator(".card-body a").last().textContent());

console.log(await page.locator(".card-body a").allTextContents());


.first()
→ Selects the first matching element.

.nth(index)
→ Selects an element based on its index.

.last()
→ Selects the last matching element.

.textContent()
→ Gets the text from the selected element.

.allTextContents()
→ Gets the text from ALL matching elements.

console.log()
→ Prints the text in the console.

--------------------------------
Why .allTextContents() Returns [] Initially but Works After .first().textContent()
--------------------------------

--------------------------------
REASON: TIMING / PAGE LOADING
--------------------------------

Example:

const allTitle = await cardTitles.allTextContents();
console.log(allTitle);

Output:

[]


Why?

→ The elements may not be loaded/present in the DOM yet.
→ `.allTextContents()` gets the text from the elements that are
  available at that moment.
→ If no matching elements are available, it returns `[]`.


--------------------------------
WHEN .first().textContent() IS USED
--------------------------------

console.log(await cardTitles.first().textContent());

const allTitle = await cardTitles.allTextContents();
console.log(allTitle);


What happens?

.first().textContent()
→ Waits for the first matching element to be available.
→ During this time, the page/elements may finish loading.
→ Now the other matching elements may also be available.
→ Therefore, allTextContents() can return the values.


Example Output:

[
    'iphone X',
    'Samsung',
    'Nokia'
]


--------------------------------
IMPORTANT POINT
--------------------------------

.first() is NOT required before .allTextContents().

.allTextContents()
→ Can directly get text from multiple matching elements.

If it returns:

[]

the likely reason is that the elements were not available
when the method was executed.


--------------------------------
CORRECT APPROACH
--------------------------------

If the elements are dynamically loaded, wait for an expected
element before getting all the text:

await expect(cardTitles.first()).toBeVisible();

const allTitle = await cardTitles.allTextContents();

console.log(allTitle);


--------------------------------
EASY WAY TO REMEMBER
--------------------------------

allTextContents()
    ↓
Gets text from currently matching elements

[]
    ↓
No matching elements were available at that moment

first().textContent()
    ↓
Waits for the first matching element

IMPORTANT:
The issue is usually TIMING / SYNCHRONIZATION,
not that allTextContents() requires first().

--------------------------------
INDEXING WITH .nth()
--------------------------------

Playwright uses zero-based indexing.

nth(0) → 1st element
nth(1) → 2nd element
nth(2) → 3rd element
nth(3) → 4th element
   ...


Example:

page.locator(".card-body a").nth(1)

→ Selects the 2nd matching element.


--------------------------------
EASY WAY TO REMEMBER
--------------------------------

.first()
    → First element

.nth(1)
    → Second element

.last()
    → Last element

.allTextContents()
    → Text from ALL matching elements
	
--------------------------------
Understanding [] with .allTextContents()
--------------------------------

.allTextContents()
→ Returns the text of ALL matching elements as an Array.

Example:

console.log(await page.locator(".card-body a").allTextContents());

Output:

[ 'iphone X', 'Samsung', 'Nokia' ]

Why []?

[] represents an Array.

Since .allTextContents() gets text from multiple elements, Playwright returns the results inside an array.

Multiple elements
       ↓
allTextContents()
       ↓
Array
       ↓
[ 'iphone X', 'Samsung', 'Nokia' ]

Accessing individual values

const products = await page.locator(".card-body a").allTextContents();

console.log(products[0]); // iphone X
console.log(products[1]); // Samsung
console.log(products[2]); // Nokia

products[0] → 1st value
products[1] → 2nd value
products[2] → 3rd value

Easy way to remember:

textContent()
    → One text value

allTextContents()
    → Multiple text values
    → Returned as an Array []

--------------------------------
UNDERSTANDING .card-body a USING HTML
--------------------------------

Example HTML:

<div class="card-body">

    <h4 class="card-title">
        <a href="#">iphone X</a>
    </h4>

    <h5>$24.99</h5>

    <p class="card-text">
        Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipisicing elit.
    </p>

</div>


--------------------------------
1. UNDERSTAND THE HTML STRUCTURE
--------------------------------

Think of the HTML structure as a tree:

div  ← class="card-body"
│
├── h4  ← class="card-title"
│    │
│    └── a
│         └── "iphone X"
│
├── h5
│    └── "$24.99"
│
└── p  ← class="card-text"
     └── "Lorem ipsum..."


The <a> element is inside the <h4>.

The <h4> is inside the <div class="card-body">.


--------------------------------
2. WHAT DOES .card-body MEAN?
--------------------------------

.card-body

The "." represents a class.

.card-body

means:

"Find the element having class='card-body'."


In the HTML:

<div class="card-body">

So:

.card-body
    ↓
Selects the <div> with class="card-body".


--------------------------------
3. WHAT DOES a MEAN?
--------------------------------

a

represents the <a> (anchor/link) element.

Example:

<a href="#">iphone X</a>


So:

a
    ↓
Finds an <a> element.


--------------------------------
4. WHAT DOES .card-body a MEAN?
--------------------------------

.card-body a

There is a SPACE between:

.card-body
     and
a


The space means:

"Find an <a> element inside .card-body."


Structure:

.card-body
    ↓
   h4
    ↓
    a


Therefore:

.card-body a

selects:

<a href="#">iphone X</a>


--------------------------------
5. IMPORTANT — DESCENDANT
--------------------------------

The <a> does NOT have to be a direct child of .card-body.

Example:

.card-body
    ↓
   h4
    ↓
    a


.card-body a

can find the <a> because <a> is a descendant of .card-body.


In simple words:

SPACE
    ↓
Means "inside / descendant"


--------------------------------
6. USING IT IN PLAYWRIGHT
--------------------------------

Example:

page.locator(".card-body a")


Meaning:

"Locate the <a> element anywhere inside the element
having class='card-body'."


Example:

console.log(
    await page.locator(".card-body a").textContent()
);


Output:

iphone X


--------------------------------
7. .card-body a vs .card-body > a
--------------------------------

.card-body a

→ Finds an <a> anywhere inside .card-body.


.card-body > a

→ Finds an <a> only if it is a DIRECT CHILD of .card-body.


Your HTML:

<div class="card-body">
    <h4>
        <a>iphone X</a>
    </h4>
</div>


Here:

.card-body a
    ↓
    ✅ Works


.card-body > a
    ↓
    ❌ Does NOT match

because <a> is not a direct child of <div>.

The <h4> is between them.


--------------------------------
8. EASY WAY TO REMEMBER
--------------------------------

.parent child

    ↓

Child can be anywhere inside the parent.


.parent > child

    ↓

Child must be a DIRECT child of the parent.


--------------------------------
FINAL EXAMPLE
--------------------------------

HTML:

<div class="card-body">
    <h4 class="card-title">
        <a href="#">iphone X</a>
    </h4>
</div>


Locator:

page.locator(".card-body a")


Means:

