--------------------------------
Playwright Waiting Methods — High-Level Explanation
--------------------------------

await page.waitForLoadState('networkidle');
await page.locator('.card-body b').waitFor();
await page.locator('.card-body b').waitFor({ state: 'visible' });
await page.locator('.card-body b').first().waitFor();
await page.locator('.card-body b').last().waitFor();

1. page.waitForLoadState('networkidle')
await page.waitForLoadState('networkidle');

→ Waits until the page reaches the network idle state.

In simple words:

Wait until there are no or very few active network connections for a short period.

Useful when the page loads data dynamically.

2. .locator().waitFor()
await page.locator('.card-body b').waitFor();

→ Waits for the first matching element to be attached to the DOM.

In simple words:

Wait until the product element exists on the page.

3. waitFor({ state: 'visible' })'
await page.locator('.card-body b').waitFor({ state: 'visible' });

→ Waits until the first matching element is visible.

In simple words:

Wait until the product element is actually visible to the user.

4. .first().waitFor()
await page.locator('.card-body b').first().waitFor();

→ Selects the first matching element and waits for it to be attached to the DOM.

In simple words:

Wait specifically for the first product element.

5. .last().waitFor()
await page.locator('.card-body b').last().waitFor();

→ Selects the last matching element and waits for it to be attached to the DOM.

In simple words:

Wait specifically for the last product element.

Easy Way to Remember

waitForLoadState()
    ↓
Wait for page loading/network activity

locator().waitFor()
    ↓
Wait for element to exist

waitFor({ state: 'visible' })
    ↓
Wait for element to be visible

.first().waitFor()
    ↓
Wait for first matching element

.last().waitFor()
    ↓
Wait for last matching element

Important: Playwright already has built-in auto-waiting for most actions and assertions, 
so you don't always need to manually add these waits.